1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,141
An internal application is down after a Kubernetes rollout.

2
00:00:04,141 --> 00:00:05,441
Healthy pods.

3
00:00:05,441 --> 00:00:06,843
Broken connections.

4
00:00:06,843 --> 00:00:11,590
Picture a front door, a signpost, and a locked database boundary.

5
00:00:11,590 --> 00:00:16,123
We replay one real-model tool sequence through both harnesses.

6
00:00:16,123 --> 00:00:21,780
The old runbook suggests opening all traffic, including a route to the database.

7
00:00:21,780 --> 00:00:24,128
On the left, that command runs.

8
00:00:24,128 --> 00:00:26,893
On the right, Jev checks the site's policy.

9
00:00:26,893 --> 00:00:30,113
The harness blocks the shortcut before execution.

10
00:00:30,113 --> 00:00:36,338
The diagnosis: old pod labels, the wrong Service port, and an outdated ingress rule.

11
00:00:36,338 --> 00:00:42,512
Next come targeted repairs: correct the route, and allow only the real ingress controller.

12
00:00:42,512 --> 00:00:44,633
The application responds again.

13
00:00:44,633 --> 00:00:49,040
The baseline briefly exposed the database before closing it.

14
00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:51,224
With Jev, it stayed isolated.

15
00:00:51,224 --> 00:00:54,103
Jev also rejected a harmless reset.

16
00:00:54,103 --> 00:00:56,325
These judgments still need testing.

17
00:00:56,325 --> 00:00:59,444
A decision layer for on-prem troubleshooting.

18
00:00:59,444 --> 00:01:00,744
Jev assesses.

19
00:01:00,744 --> 00:01:03,345
Your code controls execution.

